take make use and dispose for decades  this is how companies have been taking  raw materials and transforming them into  products purchased by consumers who  ultimately toss them out but now people  are challenging this linear model and  urging the so-called circular economy so  how is a circular economy different from  recycling the two ideas are related but  a circular economy is more systemic and  ambitious than a linear one most  recyclable products in a linear economy  ultimately get downgraded into waste but  in a circular economy no new material  input is needed that leads to less waste  and ultimately reduced costs the linear  economy often leads to a system that is  inefficient and costly and depletes  natural resources a byproduct of this is  material waste which takes up space and  may include contaminants threatening  biodiversity but still making a  production cycle fully self-sufficient  is virtually impossible some new input  will always be necessary some wastes  will always be created building a  circular economy also requires High  upfront costs  and some say a circular economy will  only ultimately delay the negative  environmental effects of the linear  economy consumers are largely  responsible for pushing organizations  towards the circular economy and can get  companies who invest in more circular  Supply chains for example SC Johnson and  son and Unilever are developing  refillable packages for cleaning  products and laundry detergents  governments also have a hand making  cross-border commitments and developing  action plans to move toward a more  circular economy so slowly we're seeing  people become more and more conscious  and they are challenging the  sustainability of the linear economy  model  foreign
 

